{"crc":"8de97bcb","kind":"Candidate","payload":{"response":"It seems like there is a misunderstanding in the issue description and the provided files. The files you've shared are related to geographic data (DBF, PRJ, SHP, SHX) and do not contain any JSONField or Django admin code that would need modification.\n\nIf the issue is about displaying JSONField values properly in the Django admin when they are readonly, this problem typically arises from how Django handles JSONField serialization. The solution involves modifying the `display_for_field` function in `django.contrib.admin.utils`.\n\nHere's how you can fix it:\n\n# path: django/contrib/admin/utils.py\n```\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\ndef display_for_field(value, field):\n    if isinstance(field, models.JSONField):\n        return json.dumps(value)\n    # ... (rest of the code remains unchanged)\n=======\ndef display_for_field(value, field):\n    if isinstance(field, models.JSONField):\n        try:\n            return json.dumps(value, indent=4)\n        except ValueError as e:\n            return str(e)  # or handle it in a way that makes sense for your application\n    # ... (rest of the code remains unchanged)\n>>>>>>> REPLACE\n```\n\nThis change ensures that when a JSONField is readonly in the admin, its value is properly serialized to a JSON string with indentation for better readability. If there's an issue with the JSON data (e.g., it's not valid), it will catch the `ValueError` and return the error message instead of crashing.\n\nMake sure to back up your original file before making changes and test this in a development environment to ensure it works as expected without breaking other parts of your application.","round":1,"slot":0},"producer":{"instance":"01KZSBHF8RJ0G49S7A6N2HTMPW","kind":"drafter","parent":"01KZSBHF8Q4K5RADT4QA3YJXQF"},"schema":"Candidate@1","seq":14,"t":1786483735.6835132}
{"crc":"afd332cf","kind":"substrate.TriggerFired","payload":{"factory":"validator","firing_key":null,"input_sha256":"sha256:284102fa95cabcc6afa7bd2fe4b7842f79d93de041c37a2a3848f27f5f80d7f9","instance":"01KZSBMZ43NQV17S6GEPGYM199","resolved_input":{"response":"It seems like there is a misunderstanding in the issue description and the provided files. The files you've shared are related to geographic data (DBF, PRJ, SHP, SHX) and do not contain any JSONField or Django admin code that would need modification.\n\nIf the issue is about displaying JSONField values properly in the Django admin when they are readonly, this problem typically arises from how Django handles JSONField serialization. The solution involves modifying the `display_for_field` function in `django.contrib.admin.utils`.\n\nHere's how you can fix it:\n\n# path: django/contrib/admin/utils.py\n```\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\ndef display_for_field(value, field):\n    if isinstance(field, models.JSONField):\n        return json.dumps(value)\n    # ... (rest of the code remains unchanged)\n=======\ndef display_for_field(value, field):\n    if isinstance(field, models.JSONField):\n        try:\n            return json.dumps(value, indent=4)\n        except ValueError as e:\n            return str(e)  # or handle it in a way that makes sense for your application\n    # ... (rest of the code remains unchanged)\n>>>>>>> REPLACE\n```\n\nThis change ensures that when a JSONField is readonly in the admin, its value is properly serialized to a JSON string with indentation for better readability. If there's an issue with the JSON data (e.g., it's not valid), it will catch the `ValueError` and return the error message instead of crashing.\n\nMake sure to back up your original file before making changes and test this in a development environment to ensure it works as expected without breaking other parts of your application.","round":1,"slot":0},"trigger_id":"validate"},"producer":null,"schema":"substrate.TriggerFired@1","seq":15,